.TH POINTER 3
.SH NAME
pointer \- mouse and stylus interface
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B "bind -a #m /dev"
.PP
.B /dev/cursor
.br
.B /dev/fingers
.br
.B /dev/pointer
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I pointer
device is the interface to input from a mouse, stylus, or some other device
for pointing at positions on a display.
On some platforms, the pointer is associated with a cursor
on the display, that is displayed at the current pointer position.
There often are buttons associated with the pointer, whether on
the pointing device or elsewhere.
For instance, some of the buttons on a PDA might be associated
with the stylus not a keypad, to allow modification of stylus actions
when there are are no buttons on the stylus itself.
In the hosted environment of
.IR emu (1),
the pointer is associated with the host system's own pointing device.
.PP
Reading the
.B pointer
file returns the current pointer position and the state of any buttons associated with the pointer.
The read blocks until the state has changed since the last read.
Each read returns a 49 byte record:
.IP
.BI m "x y buttons msec"
.PP
Each record has the letter
.B m
immedlately followed by four 11-character decimal numbers, each number ended by a blank.
.I X
and
.I y
give the coordinates of the pointer in display coordinates;
the value
.I buttons
has bit
.RI "(1<<" b )
set when button
.I b
is down; and
.I msec
is a time stamp in milliseconds.
.PP
Writing a record of the above form to
.B pointer
sends that pointer event, as if from the device:
.I buttons
held at
.I x
and
.IR y ;
.I msec
is ignored.
Under
.IR emu (1)
each such write is delivered as an event of its own, motion included,
in the order written, where the host's own pointer reports only its
latest position between changes of buttons; a write waits if events
are waiting to be read.
So a press, a move and a release written one after another reach the
reader as a drag.
The host's pointer is not moved.
A record with only
.I x
and
.I y
moves the pointer there, the host's too, with the buttons as they are.
.PP
A touch screen's fingers, every one, are read from
.BR fingers ,
an event a read:
.IP
.BI f "id x y on msec"
.PP
in the fixed-width fields
.B pointer
uses.
.I On
is 1 while the finger is on the screen (as it lands, and as it moves)
and 0 when it lifts;
.I id
is the finger's from the event it lands until the one it lifts, a small
number free again after.
.B Pointer
is the first finger as a mouse, for every program that reads it; a
program that needs every finger, a keyboard on the screen typed with
two thumbs whose touches overlap
.RI ( wm-osk (1)),
reads
.B fingers
as well.  One program reads it at a time; while none does, touches are
dropped, not kept for a reader who comes later.  A write of
.BI f "id x y on"
is such an event, from a driver that is a program (the native kernel's
touch panel,
.BR os/init/touch.b )
or a test; a write waits for room in the queue, where an event from
the GUI backend, which may not wait, is dropped (room is kept for
fingers lifting).
On the native kernel a write of
.BI a "id x y on"
is the same with
.I x
and
.I y
fractions of the screen, 0 to 65535, as
.B pointer
takes
.BI a "x y buttons"
there: a USB touchscreen's driver knows where on its surface it is
touched but not how big the screen is, and the kernel scales them.
Either kind of touch screen's driver makes the pointer of its fingers
by
.IR touchgest (2):
a finger held still is button 3, two dragged the wheel.
.PP
Writing an
.IR image (6)
file to the
.B cursor
file sets the image of the cursor on the display.
Reading
.B cursor
returns the current cursor image, again in
.IR image (6)
format.
.SH SOURCE
.B /emu/port/devpointer.c
.br
.B /os/port/devpointer.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR wm (1),
.IR draw-intro (2),
.IR touchgest (2),
.IR image (6)
